  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe warm front will lift north on Thursday. How far this \nfeature lifts north is still up in the air with this morning's \ndeterministic models. The NAM this morning is least aggressive \npushing this feature north into far southern Nebraska by \nThursday afternoon. The GFS soln has this feature well into the \nnorthern Sandhills and the EC soln from last night has this \nfeature over the central Sandhills. That being said, this \nfrontal boundary will have a huge impact on highs Thursday and \nprecipitation chances Thursday afternoon/night across the area. \nThis difference is reflected in the latest high temp guidance \nfor LBF Thursday. The MAV, MET and EC guidance has a high \ntemperature for LBF Thursday of 92, 73 and 85 degrees \nrespectively. The latest NBM has a high of 83 for LBF which is \nright in between the warmer MAV and coolest MET guidance. The \nNBM ensemble has a 11 degree spread in highs for LBF Thursday \nwith the 25th and 75th%le ranging from 78 to 89 degrees. This is\na 2 degree increase from the spread of 9 degrees (81-90) in \nlast night's forecast. For now feel the 83 degree mark is \nprobably the best course of action given the increasing spread \nin ensemble highs for Thursday. Another forecast aspect that \nhinges on frontal location is precipitation chances Thursday \nafternoon into Thursday night. All three deterministic solns \ndevelop heavy rain on north of the frontal boundary. The \nlocation of greatest QPF and best precipitation chances, ranges \nfrom the central and northern Sandhills (NAM soln), to far \nnorthern Nebraska and South Dakota EC and GFS solns. Looking at \nthe latest QPF forecast and NBM pop forecast, the northern solns\nare favored here with likely to low end categorical pops \nThursday night into Friday morning. With respect to heavy rain \npotential Thursday night, the latest EC EFI and SOT forecast is \nindicating an EFI of .8-.9 and a SOT of 1-2 over southern South \nDakota and far northern Nebraska Thursday night. Forecast PWATS \nin the NAM and GFS solns reach 1.7\"+ and 1.5\"+ Thursday \nafternoon which is well above the 99% ile for September 17th. \nThat being said, will probably mention a threat for heavy rain \nover northern Nebraska Thursday night into Friday morning in the\nHWO. Upper level low pressure will slowly track east from Idaho\nto eastern Wyoming and eastern Montana Friday into Sunday. \nPersistent southwesterly flow aloft will lead to a continued \nthreat for showers and thunderstorms across the area. ATTM the \nbest chances for widespread precipitation appear to be Saturday \nnight. Beyond Sunday, there will be a continued threat for \nshowers and thunderstorms across the area, however, coverage \nwill be spotty at best.",
